$DIR/main.p:4:1: //pu:extern function strlen must not have a body
    //pu:extern says that the function is written in C or assembly and linked in
    under the given symbol. A function with that directive must be declared
    without a body.
    (see "Assembly and C" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.4)
    (run "pluto explain E2510" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:7:1: //pu:extern function puts: parameter 1: string has no C equivalent; pass unsafe.StringData(s) and len(s)
    Functions marked //pu:extern or //pu:export are called with the C calling
    convention. Their parameters and results must be types C has: integers,
    floats, bools, pointers, and structs and arrays of those. Strings, slices,
    interfaces and func values have no C equivalent, and variadic functions are
    not supported.
    Pass a pointer and a length instead of a string or slice.
    Pass a pointer and a length:
        //pu:extern write
        func write(fd int32, buf *byte, n uint64) int64

        write(1, unsafe.SliceData(b), uint64(len(b)))
    (see "Assembly and C" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.4)
    (run "pluto explain E2511" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:13:1: //pu:export plu_add: main.add and main.add2 are both exported under one symbol
    Two functions are exported under the same symbol name with //pu:export, or
    the name is already used by another symbol. The linker needs each symbol
    name to be unique.
    (see "Assembly and C" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.4)
    (run "pluto explain E2512" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:16:1: cannot export generic function sum
    A generic function is compiled once for each set of type arguments it is
    used with, so there is no single function to export under one C symbol.
    Export a non-generic wrapper that calls it with the types you need.
    Export a wrapper:
        //pu:export sum_ints
        func sumInts(p *int, n int) int { return Sum[int](unsafe.Slice(p, n)) }
    (see "Assembly and C" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.4)
    (run "pluto explain E2513" for a longer explanation)
